-- title:
--   Gauss prime of the j-chart algebra above q
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ and $\ell'$ be primes, $M'$ a nonzero natural number, $L$ a field of characteristic $0$, and $A_0\subseteq L$ a valuation subring which is a discrete valuation ring and whose maximal ideal contains the image of $q$. Let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L(\!(\mathsf q)\!)$ obtained as `laurentBaseChange`, i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field `xHFunctionField` of level $(q\ell')^2M'$ and subgroup `levelH` $(q\ell')\,M'$ — the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb Z/(q\ell')^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/(q\ell'))^\times$ — with $K$ an $A_0$-algebra compatibly with $L$, and let $j\in K$, nonzero, have Laurent series the coefficientwise image of $j_{\mathsf q}=\mathsf q^{-1}\cdot j_{\mathrm{Num}}$. Write $C=$ `chartAlgFin` $A_0\,K\,j$ for the subalgebra of elements of $K$ integral over $A_0[j]$. Then there is a prime ideal $G$ of $C$ together with a valuation subring $W_0$ of $K$ such that: $f\in W_0$ exactly when $f\cdot y=x$ in $L(\!(\mathsf q)\!)$ for some power series $x,y$ over $A_0$ with $y$ having nonzero reduction modulo the maximal ideal; $h\in G$ exactly when $h$, viewed in $K$, is a non-unit of $W_0$; every $h\in C$ all of whose $\mathsf q$-coefficients come from the maximal ideal of $A_0$ lies in $G$; the image of $q$ lies in $G$; and for every polynomial $f$ over $A_0$ with $f(j)\in G$, all coefficients of $f$ lie in the maximal ideal of $A_0$.
--
--   This produces the Gauss prime above $q$ on the $j$-finite chart of the integral model of the full-level modular curve of level $(q\ell')^2M'$, pinned as the contraction of the maximal ideal of the Gauss valuation ring in its power-series presentation; the last clause says that $j$ is residually transcendental at $G$. It is used in the construction of the place of the chart algebra whose residue data identify the Tate point.
